DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers secondary scholarship that critically assesses, defends, compares, or situates Dussel's critique of Habermas. The literature falls into several clusters. First, there is direct secondary commentary on the Dussel–Habermas–Apel exchange, including Sorensen's analysis of Dussel's critique of discourse ethics as ideology critique, and Apel's own response to liberation philosophy in the pages of Philosophy and Social Criticism. Second, there is comparative scholarship that places Dussel's critique alongside other non-European or non-Frankfurtian critiques of Habermas—including feminist critiques (Meehan), communitarian critiques (Papastephanou on Habermas and Eurocentrism), and decolonial-theory critiques (Naicker, Radhakrishnan). Third, the volume edited by Allen and Mendieta, Decolonizing Ethics (2021), represents the most sustained recent scholarly engagement, assembling multiple authors who assess Dussel's relationship to Frankfurt School critical theory, including Allen's own chapter on Dussel and the Frankfurt School's concept of progress. The Irvine (2011) article in Sophia provides the most direct ontological critique of Dussel's own philosophical framework, questioning whether his "trans-ontology" genuinely escapes Western metaphysics.

A notable tension runs through this domain: scholars sympathetic to Dussel tend to treat his liberation ethics as a necessary supplement or corrective to discourse ethics; critics (Naicker, Irvine) argue that decolonial and liberation-philosophy frameworks often replicate the essentialisms they purport to overcome. The Quintana-Paz critique reveals internal tensions in Dussel's own criteria of liberation when applied to political violence.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the meta-level evaluative dimension the project requires: it gathers secondary voices that assess whether Dussel's critique of Habermas succeeds, where it falls short, and how it compares to feminist, postcolonial, and intra-Frankfurt criticisms of Habermasian universalism. It thereby enables the project's synthesis phase to present a balanced, critically informed account rather than simply reporting the Dussel–Habermas positions.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Direct defenses of Habermas specifically against Dussel's charge of Eurocentrism are underrepresented in English-language secondary literature; most defenses of Habermasian universalism take the feminist (Fraser, Benhabib) or communitarian (Taylor) challenges as primary interlocutors rather than the liberation-philosophy critique. The Sorensen article (Public Reason, 2010) does not have a CrossRef DOI and could not be verified through CrossRef; the URL at publicreason.ro was used for identification.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The synthesis should foreground the Allen–Mendieta volume as the most comprehensive scholarly intervention connecting Dussel's ethics to Frankfurt School debates. The Naicker and Radhakrishnan papers provide the dissenting/critical voices needed to balance the account; the Irvine and Quintana-Paz pieces supply more internal critiques of Dussel's own framework.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Sympathetic assessment (Dussel supplements Frankfurt School): 5 papers — Allen, Mendieta, Mills, Sorensen, Maldonado-Torres
- Critical/dissenting view of decolonial/liberation frameworks: 3 papers — Naicker, Irvine, Quintana-Paz
- Comparative (feminist, European): 3 papers — Meehan, Papastephanou, Radhakrishnan
- Comparative South-South: 1 paper — Guennouni Idrissi
====================================================================
}

@incollection{allen2021ethics,
  author = {Allen, Amy},
  title = {The Ethics and Politics of Progress: Dussel and the Frankfurt School},
  booktitle = {Decolonizing Ethics: The Critical Theory of Enrique Dussel},
  editor = {Allen, Amy and Mendieta, Eduardo},
  publisher = {Penn State University Press},
  year = {2021},
  pages = {167--181},
  doi = {10.1515/9780271090320-010},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Allen argues that Dussel's liberation ethics and the Frankfurt School share a structural commitment to critique in the name of those excluded by the prevailing system, but that Dussel's emphasis on the material suffering of victims challenges the Habermasian and Honnethian assumption that immanent norms of recognition or communicative reason are sufficient normative grounds. Allen contends that Dussel's transmodern perspective exposes a Eurocentric concept of progress embedded in Frankfurt School critical theory.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most direct scholarly intervention on the specific question of Dussel versus the Frankfurt School, written by one of the leading English-language interpreters of Habermas. It demonstrates both convergences and fundamental tensions and provides a balanced framework for the overall synthesis.

  POSITION: Sympathetic critical engagement; treats liberation ethics as a necessary supplement to immanent Frankfurt School critique.
  },
  keywords = {Dussel, Frankfurt School, liberation ethics, progress, coloniality, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{allenMendieta2021,
  editor = {Allen, Amy and Mendieta, Eduardo},
  title = {Decolonizing Ethics: The Critical Theory of Enrique Dussel},
  publisher = {Penn State University Press},
  year = {2021},
  doi = {10.1515/9780271090320},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This edited volume assembles leading scholars to assess Dussel's critical theory across multiple dimensions: its engagement with Frankfurt School thinkers (Habermas, Honneth, Adorno), its treatment of political violence, its feminist dimensions, and its place in the decolonial turn. The editors argue that Dussel's ethics of liberation represents a substantive and philosophically rigorous challenge to Eurocentrism within critical theory.

  RELEVANCE: As the most comprehensive recent English-language secondary treatment of Dussel's relationship to Frankfurt School critical theory, this volume is indispensable context for evaluating the reception of Dussel's critique of Habermas. Multiple chapters directly address the Habermas–Dussel exchange.

  POSITION: Collectively oriented toward sympathetic but rigorous critical engagement with Dussel's project.
  },
  keywords = {Dussel, Frankfurt School, Habermas, decolonizing, critical theory, High},
  abstract = {Enrique Dussel is Latin America’s foremost philosopher, renowned for his contributions to ethics, political philosophy, and liberation theology. Designed for classroom use, this collection of essays engages with Dussel’s encyclopedic work, making his valuable contributions accessible to English-speaking students. In addition to being one of the most original, prolific, and widely known members of the Latin American Philosophy of Liberation movement, Dussel has also made important contributions to world philosophy, the history of philosophy, the history of the Catholic Church in Latin America, and the understanding of Karl Marx. Dussel famously engaged in a decade-long debate with Karl-Otto Apel on the relationship between material and formal ethics—that is, between an ethics of the community of life and an ethics of the community of discourse—and he has produced novel interpretations and analyses of the concepts of alterity, exteriority, the other, and the world history of ethical systems. Most recently, Dussel extended his work on an ethics of liberation into a politics of liberation, developed over the course of three published volumes. In this book, scholars from around the world assess Dussel’s work in ways that are both appreciative and critical. Two essays by Dussel bookend the volume: the collection opens with a consideration of the (im)possibility of multiple modernities and ends with an autobiographical trajectory of the philosopher’s thinking. In addition to Dussel and the editors, the contributors to this volume include Linda Martín Alcoff, Don Thomas Deere, Oscar Guardiola-Rivera, Mario Sáenz Rovner, Alejandro A. Vallega, and Jorge Zúñiga M.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{apelMendieta1996,
  author = {Apel, Karl-Otto and Mendieta, Eduardo},
  title = {{`Discourse ethics' before the challenge of `liberation philosophy'}},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {1996},
  volume = {22},
  number = {2},
  pages = {1--25},
  doi = {10.1177/019145379602200201},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Apel's essay, translated and introduced by Mendieta, constitutes the main Frankfurt-tradition response to the challenge posed by liberation philosophy. Apel argues that discourse ethics is not merely formal but contains a transformative principle: the obligation to approximate ideal discourse conditions, which already implies material concern for the excluded. He denies that discourse ethics is ideologically complicit with the status quo.

  RELEVANCE: This is the closest to a direct defense of the discourse-ethics tradition against Dussel's charge of ideological formalism. As Habermas's philosophical partner in discourse ethics, Apel's response clarifies what a defense of universalist discourse ethics against liberation philosophy looks like.

  POSITION: Defense of discourse ethics as materially transformative against liberation philosophy's charge of ideological formalism.
  },
  keywords = {Apel, discourse ethics, liberation philosophy, response, defense, High},
  abstract = {Mesurant la contribution d'E. Dussel au dialogue concernant la relation entre l'ethique du discours et la philosophie de la liberation, l'A. confronte la conception d'une economie transcendantale, developpee par Dussel, a sa propre conception realiste des rapports conflictuels entre le Nord et le Sud},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{irvine2011ontological,
  author = {Irvine, Andrew B.},
  title = {An Ontological Critique of the Trans-Ontology of {Enrique Dussel}},
  journal = {Sophia},
  year = {2011},
  volume = {50},
  number = {4},
  pages = {603--624},
  doi = {10.1007/s11841-010-0210-8},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Irvine argues that Dussel's claim to have transcended Western ontology through the "analectic" method is philosophically questionable. He contends that Dussel's appeal to the exteriority of the Other remains internally dependent on categories (dialectic, negation, metaphysics) drawn from the Western tradition Dussel purports to overcome, and that this does not constitute a genuine "trans-ontology."

  RELEVANCE: This provides the most sustained philosophical critique of Dussel's own foundational moves, raising the question of whether his critique of Habermas rests on a coherent alternative philosophical framework. A synthesis of the Dussel–Habermas debate needs to assess whether Dussel's counter-position is itself philosophically defensible.

  POSITION: Internal/ontological critique of Dussel's philosophical foundations from within a broadly Western-philosophical perspective.
  },
  keywords = {Dussel, trans-ontology, analectic, critique, assessment, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{quintanaPaz2014,
  author = {Quintana-Paz, Miguel {\'A}ngel},
  title = {Enrique {Dussel} and Liberation Theology: Violence or Dialogue?},
  journal = {Journal of Interdisciplinary Studies},
  year = {2014},
  volume = {26},
  number = {1},
  pages = {135--146},
  doi = {10.5840/jis2014261/27},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Quintana-Paz argues that Dussel's ethics of liberation contains an internal contradiction: he borrows formal-discursive criteria (from Apel and Habermas) to legitimize political revolution, yet the very criteria—communicative consensus, discursive validity, feasibility—are conceptually incompatible with the use of violence that Dussel also seeks to justify. The essay exposes a tension between Dussel's procedural debts to discourse ethics and his substantive political commitments.

  RELEVANCE: This critique is directly relevant to the question of how much Dussel's liberation ethics depends on (and remains in tension with) Habermasian discourse ethics. It shows that Dussel's critique of Habermas does not free him from Habermasian normative categories.

  POSITION: Internal critique of Dussel's ethics; reveals philosophical dependence on discourse-ethics criteria while assessing the coherence of his liberation project.
  },
  keywords = {Dussel, liberation theology, violence, discourse ethics, critique, Medium},
  abstract = {For centuries, various disciplines have tried to tackle the topic of how legitimate it is to use violence in order to solve social problems. One of the most recent interdisciplinary approaches, most successful in present-day Latin America, is the so-called "Ethics of Liberation," designed by Enrique Dussel. Based on the Theology of Liberation, this theory goes beyond the limits of theology as a discipline and pleads for three ethical criteria that every political revolution must fulfill to use violence in a legitimate way. The first is a formal criterion, which basically takes after the ideal dialogue situation endorsed by Karl-Otto Apel and Jürgen Habermas, and purports to be rooted in yet another discipline, linguistics. The second is a material criterion, defined as the upshot of an acceptable welfare for all citizens, thus intimately linked with the discipline of economics and political philosophy. The third is a criterion of feasibility, which makes a revolt legitimate if, and only if, it has a reasonable possibility of succeeding; hence strategic issues take a leading role. This essay contends that each of these criteria is conceptually incompatible with violence. Hence, Dussel's arguments involve multiple contradictions as he aims to justify the use of violence precisely with these interdisciplinary criteria.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{meehan2000feminism,
  author = {Meehan, Johanna},
  title = {Feminism and {Habermas'} discourse ethics},
  journal = {Philosophy \& Social Criticism},
  year = {2000},
  volume = {26},
  number = {3},
  pages = {39--52},
  doi = {10.1177/019145370002600302},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Meehan examines feminist critiques of Habermas's discourse ethics, arguing that while feminists are often suspicious of discourse ethics for its formalism and proceduralism, many of their misgivings are misplaced. Habermas's account of the intersubjective constitution of subjectivity and his defense of rational normativity are useful for feminist theory, though his reductive identification of subjectivity with language and rationality requires revision.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the feminist comparandum for Dussel's critique: where Dussel criticizes discourse ethics for ignoring material suffering and the victims of the world system, feminists target its formalism and gendered assumptions. Comparing both critiques illuminates the range of objections to Habermasian universalism.

  POSITION: Feminist partial defense and critique of Habermas's discourse ethics; relevant as a comparative benchmark.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas, feminism, discourse ethics, universalism, comparison, Medium},
  abstract = {Habermas’ account of the radically intersubjective constitution of subjectivity is of great use to feminist theorists, as is his defense of the rational character of normative claims. Feminists must however, reject his reductive identification of subjectivity with language and rationality. Some feminists’ concerns insist on continuing to distinguish morality from legality, something that Habermas, despite his own better intuitions and arguments, is sometimes disinclined to do.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{papastephanou2011eurocentrism,
  author = {Papastephanou, Marianna},
  title = {Eurocentrism beyond the `universalism vs. particularism' dilemma: {Habermas} and {Derrida's} joint plea for a new {Europe}},
  journal = {History of the Human Sciences},
  year = {2011},
  volume = {24},
  number = {5},
  pages = {142--166},
  doi = {10.1177/0952695111422317},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Papastephanou argues that the charge of Eurocentrism against Habermas (and Derrida) cannot be adequately addressed through the standard universalism/particularism dichotomy. She examines whether Habermas and Derrida's joint 2003 plea for a European identity moves beyond Eurocentrism or reinstates it in subtler form, concluding that post-national universalism retains Eurocentric assumptions even in its most self-critical formulations.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a comparative perspective on the charge of Eurocentrism against Habermas from European scholarship—distinct from Dussel's Latin American liberation standpoint. Useful for the synthesis's treatment of how the charge of Eurocentrism appears across different critical traditions.

  POSITION: Critical from within European scholarship; neither fully defends nor fully condemns Habermas on Eurocentrism.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas, Eurocentrism, universalism, Derrida, comparative, Medium},
  abstract = {[[cosmopolitanismexpansionismidealismparticularismUNuniversalism ]] Is it Eurocentric on the part of western philosophers (Habermas, Derrida) or of researchers in human sciences to set out from a specific locality (Europe) to formulate ethico-political ideals with universal aspirations? In this article, I critique the ‘universalism vs. particularism’ framework within which the charge of Eurocentrism is deployed and I redefine the notion of Eurocentrism outside the drastic choice between universalism and particularism and in light of an ‘ec-centric’ reflection on the entanglement of the ‘We’ and the ‘others’. I illustrate my position by discussing the Habermasian–Derridean plea for the determination of new European political responsibilities beyond any Eurocentrism. I grant that the two philosophers go indeed beyond Eurocentrism as the latter is usually understood, i.e. along the axis ‘universal–particular’. Yet, without minimizing their political contribution, I detect subtler Eurocentrisms that pervade several assumptions of Habermas’s and Derrida’s collaborative efforts. I argue that a hasty enlisting of possibilities for a politically and morally pertinent European intervention in world affairs fails to account for other, more necessary steps in the direction of reforming the western consciousness and of going beyond less easily discernible Eurocentrisms such as those theorized within the proposed, reformulated notion.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{radhakrishnan2025critique,
  author = {Radhakrishnan, Shivani},
  title = {Critique as Coloniality: The Decolonial Challenge to Immanent Critique},
  journal = {Constellations},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {32},
  number = {3},
  pages = {476--483},
  doi = {10.1111/1467-8675.12790},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Radhakrishnan argues that the Frankfurt School's methodological commitment to immanent critique—evaluating social practices by their own internal norms and potentials—is structurally unable to address colonial and postcolonial critique, because it anchors normativity in the very practices that have been shaped by colonial power. The decolonial challenge requires a form of critique that steps outside the immanent norms of modernity itself.

  RELEVANCE: This article is among the most recent contributions to the meta-level question of whether Frankfurt School critical theory (including Habermas's communicative-rationality framework) can be decolonized or must be replaced. It directly contextualizes Dussel's critique within a broader decolonial challenge to critical theory.

  POSITION: Strong decolonial critique; challenges the adequacy of immanent critique as a methodology for addressing colonial legacies.
  },
  keywords = {decolonial, immanent critique, Frankfurt School, Habermas, coloniality, High},
  abstract = {Frankfurt School methodology involves a lasting commitment to immanent critique. What distinguishes immanent critique from other forms of social criticism, scholars in this tradition argue, is that social practices are to be judged according to norms and potentials already contained within their objects. This article considers critical theory's relationship to coloniality by developing a three‐part challenge to the practice of immanent critique, drawing on insights of decolonial philosophers Anibal Quijano, Enrique Dussel, Maria Lugones, and Oyèrónkẹ́ Oyěwùmí. Immanent critics, I conclude, risk being inattentive to constitutive exclusions, reinscribing asymmetrical burdens on certain critics, and unwittingly replaying a mechanism of domination familiar from colonialism in and through their preferred method of critique. Accordingly, I argue that immanent critics should attend to relations of power that govern the practice of critique itself and offer reasons for being more pluralistic about our methods of criticism.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{naicker2023decolonial,
  author = {Naicker, Veeran},
  title = {The problem of epistemological critique in contemporary {Decolonial} theory},
  journal = {Social Dynamics},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {49},
  number = {2},
  pages = {220--241},
  doi = {10.1080/02533952.2023.2226497},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Naicker critically responds to decolonial theory's reductive generalizations about Western epistemology, arguing that its articulation of geopolitics and standpoint theory amounts to a simplistic inversion of colonial stereotypes. He argues that decolonial theory misrepresents Western epistemology by reducing it to a subject-object dualism, and that decolonial theory is itself self-defeating because it replicates the metaphysical errors it attributes to Western philosophy.

  RELEVANCE: Naicker's critique of decolonial theory as a whole is directly relevant to assessing the cogency of Dussel's critique of Habermas, since Dussel's liberation ethics is one of the foundational sources for the decolonial turn. The paper represents the critical-dissenting strand in the reception of this literature.

  POSITION: Philosophical critique of decolonial theory's epistemological foundations, including the Dussel-derived strands.
  },
  keywords = {decolonial theory, epistemological critique, Eurocentrism, assessment, dissenting, Medium},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT Decolonial theory argues that the coloniality of rational Western epistemology is the cause of all global crises, including violent patriarchy, racism and ecological destruction. Contemporary decolonial scholars consequently advocate de-linking from the colonial matrix of power which defines the modern world system. They promote a move towards an epistemologically pluriversal world, which they consider to be theoretically superior and a politically radical advance over postructuralism, postcolonialism and Marxism, which are denigrated as complicit iterations of Eurocentric coloniality. In this article, I critically respond to Decolonial theory’s reductive generalisations by demonstrating that its ostensibly constructivist articulation of geopolitics and standpoint theory amounts to a simplistic inversion of colonial stereotypes found in postcolonial analyses of colonial discourse. After establishing that Decolonial theory is unwittingly bound to a colonial, albeit inverted discourse of essentialist racialised morality, I exhibit that its stereotypical reduction of Western epistemology to a subject-object dualism misrepresents developments in Euro-American epistemology and continental philosophy of science, citing the case of French historical epistemology. I conclude by demonstrating that Decolonial theory is contradictory and self-defeating because it replicates metaphysical errors in Western philosophy and supports political values closer to the far right than leftist ideals of universality and a common world.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{guennouni2025misreading,
  author = {Guennouni Idrissi, Achraf},
  title = {Misreading the {R{\=u}{\d{h}}}: {Taha Abderrahmane}, {Enrique Dussel} and the ethics of decolonial modernities},
  journal = {Postcolonial Studies},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {28},
  number = {2},
  pages = {132--155},
  doi = {10.1080/13688790.2025.2546187},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Guennouni Idrissi examines two non-European critiques of Western modernity—Dussel's from Latin America and Taha Abderrahmane's from the Islamic tradition. He argues that Dussel's pluriversal framework misreads Taha's project, treating it as abstract universalism when it is in fact a form of decolonial ethical specificity. The article shows that Dussel's critique of Western universalism can itself commit the error of undifferentiated anti-universalism.

  RELEVANCE: This South–South comparison is uniquely relevant to the project's question of whether Dussel's critique of Habermasian universalism is itself universal or parochially Latin American. It extends the comparative dimension beyond the usual Frankfurt School versus liberation ethics axis.

  POSITION: Comparative postcolonial critique; exposes limitations in Dussel's pluriversal framework from a non-Western but non-Habermasian standpoint.
  },
  keywords = {Dussel, Taha Abderrahmane, modernity, pluriversality, postcolonial comparison, Medium},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT This article explores the contrasting perspectives of Moroccan philosopher Taha Abderrahmane and Latin American thinker Enrique Dussel on the question of multiple modernities within South–South intellectual dialogue. While Dussel, grounded in the philosophy of liberation, critiques modernity as an inherently Eurocentric and colonial construct – arguing that its universalization inevitably reproduces colonial logics – Taha offers a distinctive ethical reimagining of modernity. He differentiates between the universal spirit (rūḥ) of modernity, embodied in principles, such as intellectual maturity (rushd), critique (naqd) and universality (shumūl) and its historically contingent Western expressions. Against mimicry and secular developmentalism, Taha calls for a creative engagement (ibdāʿ) with the Islamic tradition (turāth) to cultivate a spiritually grounded, ethically transformative and non-imitative modernity. This article argues that while Dussel’s critique remains incisive, it ultimately misreads Taha’s project as abstract universalism, thereby overlooking its decolonial logic and ethical specificity. By redefining modernity through al-ibdāʿ al-mawṣūl (connected creativity), Taha confronts Eurocentric dominance and unsettles the very anti-universalist premises on which Dussel’s pluriversal framework rests. His vision gestures towards a temporally decolonized modernity rooted in ethical universality and pluriversal coexistence.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{mills2018dussel,
  author = {Mills, Frederick B.},
  title = {Enrique {Dussel's} {Ethics} of {Liberation}: {An} {Introduction}},
  publisher = {Springer International Publishing},
  year = {2018},
  doi = {10.1007/978-3-319-94550-7},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Mills provides a systematic introduction to Dussel's ethics of liberation, tracing its development across Dussel's major works and situating it in relation to Levinas, Marx, Apel, and Habermas. The book reconstructs Dussel's three material, formal, and feasibility principles and shows how the critique of discourse ethics fits within the larger architectonic of his liberation ethics.

  RELEVANCE: As an accessible secondary overview, Mills's book is important for the synthesis phase as it contextualizes Dussel's specific critiques of Habermas within his broader philosophical project. It also discusses how Dussel's integration of discourse-ethics elements (the formal-discursive principle) complicates simple opposition between the two thinkers.

  POSITION: Expository and interpretive introduction, broadly sympathetic to Dussel's project.
  },
  keywords = {Dussel, liberation ethics, introduction, Habermas, Apel, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{ahmad2025habermas,
  author = {Ahmad, Irfan},
  title = {{Habermas} as an ethnic thinker {Par Excellence}: on critique, {Palestine} and the role of intellectuals},
  journal = {Teaching in Higher Education},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {30},
  number = {7},
  pages = {1343--1362},
  doi = {10.1080/13562517.2025.2466001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ahmad argues that Habermas's claims to universalism are structurally ethnic rather than genuinely universal—oriented toward delivery "to all" rather than construction "from all." Using Habermas's 2023 statement on the Israel-Palestine conflict as a test case, Ahmad contends that Habermas's universalism replicates a Eurocentric-Enlightenment framework that excludes non-European voices from the standpoint of critique.

  RELEVANCE: While not focused on Dussel specifically, this recent (2025) critique of Habermas's universalism closely parallels Dussel's central charge and serves as evidence that the debate about Habermasian universalism and its blind spots continues to generate new scholarship. It provides a current, non-Latin-American version of the Eurocentrism critique.

  POSITION: Strong external critique of Habermas's universalism as structurally ethnic; parallel to but independent of Dussel's critique.
  },
  keywords = {Habermas, universalism, Eurocentrism, critique, postcolonial, Low},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT Taking Habermas’ 2023 statement on Palestinians-Israel as the point of entry, this article examines his concept of critique. Against the dominant view of him as a philosopher of ‘universalism’ and ‘critical rationality,’ my thesis is that Habermas is an ethnic thinker, for, his ideas of critique and universalism unidirectionally rest on ‘to all’ rather than ‘from all.’ Consequently, it is missionary and borders on Islamophobia, particularly after 9/11. I show how Habermas’ denial of Palestinians’ genocide and his unqualified support to ‘Israel's right to exist’ as integral to Germany's ‘democratic ethos’ is neither an ample departure from his participation in the Hitler Youth nor from his understanding of the Enlightenment-modernity but largely their offshoots. I also juxtapose Habermas’ role as a public intellectual with that of Imam Malik (d. 795) who chose to be flogged rather than parrot unjust language of power elites. I conclude with three broad implications this article has in the field of teaching in higher education.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}
